WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 40: The Marquis de Mores and the Beef Empire of Medora

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.440
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:06.720
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:17.840
[Rebecca] In the late 19th century, the Badlands of Western North Dakota were one of the most remote, rugged wildernesses in North America, a maze of sculpted buttes, deep coolies, and howling prairie winds.

4
00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:23.360
[Rebecca] But in 1883, this untamed frontier was invaded by an aristocratic whirlwind.

5
00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:44.400
[Rebecca] Armed with millions of dollars in Wall Street banking wealth from his American heiress wife, the Marquis wore custom gray corduroy coats, silver spurs, and twin-holstered pistols, dreaming of revolutionizing the American livestock industry.

6
00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:56.640
[Rebecca] In just three frenzied years, he founded the town of Medora, built a pioneering refrigerated beef packing empire, fought deadly gun duels, and almost killed a young fellow rancher named Theodore Roosevelt.

7
00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:01.360
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story.

8
00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:26.800
[George] Stepping onto the rough pine platform was Anton Amy Marie Vincent de Valambrosa, the Marquis de Mores, a dashing six-foot-to-25-year-old French nobleman and former cavalry officer of the prestigious Saint Cyr Military Academy.

9
00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:43.520
[George] Possessing aristocratic bloodlines stretching back centuries, the Marquis sported a waxed black handlebar mustache, a dueling scar across his cheek, custom gray corduroy riding coats with silver buttons, and polished leather boots fitted with Mexican silver spurs.

10
00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:52.400
[George] He was famously lethal with both the rapier and the dueling pistol, having already killed two fellow French officers on the field of honor in Paris.

11
00:01:52.400 --> 00:02:07.400
[George] The Marquis had recently married Medorah von Hoffmann, the stunning, highly educated daughter of Louis von Hoffmann, one of the founding partners of Wall Street's powerhouse investment banking firms, armed with limitless millions in banking credit.

12
00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:15.160
[George] The young nobleman looked out over the untamed Dakota Badlands and recognized a glaring inefficiency in the American beef industry.

13
00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:30.760
[George] Western cattlemen raised steers on northern plains, then crammed them into suffocating rail cars for a grueling two-week journey to Chicago slaughterhouses, during which the animals lost dozens of pounds of meat from stress, dehydration, and bruising.

14
00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:34.440
[George] The Marquis conceived an audacious revolutionary plan.

15
00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:48.440
[George] Slaughter cattle locally in the Badlands, pack the dressed beef into newly invented refrigerated rail cars, and ship chilled beef directly to eastern butcher shops, completely bypassing and destroying the Chicago meatpacking trust.

16
00:02:48.440 --> 00:03:01.880
[George] The Badlands rose like ancient medieval castles of clay, lignite coal, and red scoria along the little Missouri River, creating a surreal labyrinth of coolies and bluffs where bighorn sheep and buffalo roamed.

17
00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:09.960
[George] With millions in Wall Street banking capital behind him, the Marquis moved with dizzying military speed and imperial grandeur.

18
00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:22.600
[George] On April 1, 1883, he pitched a silk wall tent on the east bank of the little Missouri River and christened his new commercial kingdom, Medora, named in honor of his beloved American bride.

19
00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:31.800
[George] Within months, hundreds of carpenters, stonemasons and engineers transformed the empty sagebrush flat into a booming frontier Company town.

20
00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:43.560
[George] The Marquis constructed a massive, state of the art brick slaughterhouse equipped with advanced ammonia refrigeration units, steam boilers, blood-drying fertilizers, and electric dynamos.

21
00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:57.200
[George] He constructed the Rough Riders Hotel, a Catholic brick chapel for Medora, a general store, a brickmaking kiln, and a wholesale cold storage distribution network with depots stretching from Duluth to Seattle.

22
00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:12.160
[George] High on a bluff overlooking the town, he erected the Château de Moores, an opulent 26-room, two-story wooden palace surrounded by broad verandas, staffed with French chefs, liveried servants, and imported European wine sellers.

23
00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:31.680
[George] Madame de Mores, an accomplished pianist, linguist and crack marksman who could shoot grouse on the wing while galloping side saddle in a velvet riding habit, entertained traveling European nobility, British lords and territorial governors with vintage champagne while prairie wolves howled outside the parlor windows.

24
00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:44.320
[George] The Marquis arrived in the Dakota frontier dressed in custom London corduroy, patent leather hunting boots, and a wide-brimmed felt hat, accompanied by a retinue of French valets and fine sporting rifles.

25
00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:54.080
[George] The Marquis's aristocratic arrogance and aggressive land claims quickly ignited bitter hatred among the seasoned frontier cowboys and small cattlemen of the Badlands.

26
00:04:54.080 --> 00:05:01.760
[George] The Marquis fenced off vast tracts of open public grazing land, claiming that open range was an uncivilized relic.

27
00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:06.000
[George] Tensions exploded into armed violence in June 1883.

28
00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:18.560
[George] A group of rough local frontiersmen, led by a violent drunk named Frank O'Donnell, repeatedly cut the Marquis's barbed wire fences, threatened to kill the Frenchman, and ambushed his cowboys.

29
00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:27.040
[George] The Marquis, refusing to be intimidated, notified the local sheriff in Mandan and legally purchased Winchester rifles for his men.

30
00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:37.480
[George] On June 26, while riding through a narrow coulee outside Medora, the Marquis and his ranch hands were ambushed by O'Donnell's gang firing from behind a ridge.

31
00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:41.320
[George] The Marquis and his men returned fire with deadly precision.

32
00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:45.960
[George] A cowboy named Riley Lufsey was shot through the neck and killed instantly.

33
00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:52.200
[George] The Marquis was arrested and charged with first-degree murder, spending weeks in jail in Bismarck.

34
00:05:52.200 --> 00:06:06.760
[George] Despite intense public animosity from local cattlemen who wanted to see the foreign aristocrat hanged, the Marquis conducted himself with cool, courtly dignity, and an impartial jury acquitted him on the clear grounds of justifiable self-defense.

35
00:06:06.760 --> 00:06:20.520
[George] He established refrigerated slaughterhouses right on the prairie, arguing that chilling beef immediately after slaughter preserved the natural flavor far better than shipping live emaciated cattle thousands of miles in crowded railcars.

36
00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:33.320
[George] Just months after the Marquis founded Medora, another famous easterner arrived in the Badlands seeking healing after the tragic simultaneous deaths of his wife and mother, 24-year-old Theodore Roosevelt.

37
00:06:33.320 --> 00:06:39.880
[George] Roosevelt established his own Elkhorn and Maltese Cross ranches just a few miles upriver from Medora.

38
00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:49.160
[George] At first, the two ambitious young men were polite neighbors, dining together at the chateau, but their relationship soon soured into icy tension.

39
00:06:49.160 --> 00:06:58.120
[George] Roosevelt despised the Marquis's imperious European feudal attitude, while the Marquis viewed Roosevelt as a meddling, self-righteous upstart.

40
00:06:58.120 --> 00:07:04.200
[George] The rivalry boiled over in 1885 over a dispute regarding stray cattle and fence lines.

41
00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:11.800
[George] The Marquis sent Roosevelt an icy, formal letter containing what Roosevelt interpreted as a thinly veiled challenge to a duel.

42
00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:16.880
[George] There is between us a question of principle, my business requires that I settle this.

43
00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:19.280
[George] Roosevelt was not a man to back down.

44
00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:32.400
[George] He immediately penned a furious, legendary response, stating that he was prepared to meet the Marquis anywhere, anytime, with weapons of the Marquis's choosing, suggesting Winchester rifles at twelve paces.

45
00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:46.000
[George] Faced with Roosevelt's fearless resolve, the Marquis wisely de-escalated, sending a polite reply explaining that his words had been misunderstood, and the two frontier icons remained wary, respectful acquaintances.

46
00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:57.280
[George] Roosevelt kept a loaded Winchester across his saddle horn, warning the flamboyant French aristocrat that Western ranchers would not be bullied by foreign duelists or armed range patrols.

47
00:07:57.280 --> 00:08:06.560
[George] Despite the Marquis's boundless energy and innovative ideas, his refrigerated beef empire was doomed by powerful economic and natural forces.

48
00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:17.520
[George] First, the entrenched Chicago Meatpacking Trust, controlled by Barons Gustavus Swift and Philip Armour, recognized the mortal threat posed by the Marquis's chilled beef.

49
00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:28.800
[George] The trust pressured eastern butcher shops to boycott Medora beef, and colluded with transcontinental railroad directors to charge the Marquis exorbitant freight rates for refrigerated cars.

50
00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:32.320
[George] Then, the knockout blow was delivered by Mother Nature.

51
00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:40.560
[George] The winter of 1886 and 1887 was the most catastrophic in Great Plains history, the infamous Great Die-Up.

52
00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:48.320
[George] Week after week of ferocious blizzards plunged temperatures to forty below zero, burying the badlands under ten feet of snow.

53
00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:53.600
[George] Millions of cattle starved or froze standing upright against barbed wire fences.

54
00:08:53.600 --> 00:09:03.160
[George] When the spring thaw arrived in 1887, 80% of the livestock across the northern plains was dead, their carcasses clogging the little Missouri River.

55
00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:06.920
[George] The Marquis's northern beef empire was financially obliterated.

56
00:09:07.160 --> 00:09:16.120
[George] His packing plant closed its doors forever in 1887, having lost over one and a half million dollars of his father-in-law's banking fortune.

57
00:09:16.120 --> 00:09:23.000
[George] Defeated but unbowed, the Marquis and Medora packed their belongings and returned to France in 1887.

58
00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:26.440
[George] But the Marquis was incapable of living a quiet life.

59
00:09:26.440 --> 00:09:41.320
[George] In Paris, he threw himself into anti-British political activism, founded a militant political party, and fought three more high-profile duels in the Boise de Boulogne, fatally wounding a prominent French officer in a celebrated sword fight.

60
00:09:41.320 --> 00:09:56.680
[George] In 1896, driven by an unquenchable thirst for imperial adventure, the 37-year-old Marquis led an audacious French expedition into the heart of the Sahara Desert to unite North African Muslim tribes against British rule in the Sudan.

61
00:09:56.680 --> 00:10:06.520
[George] On June 9, 1896, in the barren sand dunes near El Washa, his native Tuareg guides betrayed him, ambushing the expedition.

62
00:10:06.520 --> 00:10:17.400
[George] The Marquis fought like a lion, wielding his frontier Winchester rifle and revolvers, he killed several attackers before being overwhelmed and cut down by Tuareg spears.

63
00:10:17.400 --> 00:10:26.600
[George] The dashing French nobleman who had conquered the Badlands died as he had lived, sword in hand, under a desert sun thousands of miles from home.

64
00:10:26.600 --> 00:10:35.560
[George] Today, the little town of Medora, North Dakota stands as one of the most enchanting and impeccably preserved historic gems in the American West.

65
00:10:35.560 --> 00:10:47.840
[George] The 26-room Château de Moraes sits perched high above the river, frozen in time with its original French furnishings, hunting rifles, and family portraits, preserved as a state historic site.

66
00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:56.560
[George] The massive brick chimney of the Marquis's abandoned packing plant still rises proudly from the sagebrush against the multicolored clay bluffs.

67
00:10:56.560 --> 00:11:07.680
[George] Millions of visitors travel to Medura each year to experience the world famous Medora musical in the outdoor Burning Hills Amphitheater and to explore Theodore Roosevelt National Park.

68
00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:12.720
[George] The legacy of the Marquis de Mores remains an unforgettable frontier paradox.

69
00:11:12.720 --> 00:11:25.920
[George] A French aristocrat whose grand corporate dream crumbled in the snow, yet whose daring vision and romantic flair helped birth the modern cattle industry and forge the legendary character of the Dakota Badlands.

70
00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:38.240
[Rebecca] The Marquis de Mores and his Badlands Empire prove that the American West attracted dreamers of every station, from European nobles to frontier scouts, each leaving an indelible mark on the landscape.

71
00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:41.200
[Rebecca] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

72
00:11:41.200 --> 00:11:42.400
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

73
00:11:42.400 --> 00:11:48.480
[Rebecca] Join us next Tuesday as we witness the terrifying fury of the schoolchildren's blizzard of 1888.

74
00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:51.040
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

